WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.220
It's official: Singer/actress Halle Bailey
is earning her fins as Ariel — and now,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.250
following a bit of backlash on social media,
she's breaking her silence about being cast

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.300
in the upcoming remake of The Little Mermaid.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.100
As you may already know, Bailey is a 19-year-old
singer who performs alongside her older sister

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.760
Chloe in the R&amp;B duo Chloe x Halle.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.780
"Hey guys, it's Chloe."

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.460
"And Halle."

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.500
She's also an actress on the hit show Grown-ish.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.800
"You practice a little bit of it every day.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.520
And you know, you can't get curl and life
goals in one weekend."

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.780
"Wow, you're right."

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.620
In early July, the House of Mouse announced
that she'd been cast as Ariel in the live-action

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.840
remake of The Little Mermaid, and the news
caused something of a stir on social media.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.059
Some fans of the original expressed disappointment
over the casting choice, expressing that Bailey,

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:50.110
a black woman, wasn't the right person to
play Ariel, who is depicted as having white

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.440
skin and red hair in the 1989 animated original.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.420
Now Bailey has come forward to reveal her
thoughts on the matter.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:02.559
"Feel like I'm dreaming, and I'm just grateful,
and I don't pay attention to the negativity."

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:03.559
She added,

00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:06.700
"I just feel like this role was something
bigger than me, and greater, and it's going

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:07.400
to be beautiful.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.180
I'm just so excited to be a part of it."

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.340
In a separate Variety interview, Bailey admitted
that,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.800
"[Taking on the role] is definitely a little
bit nerve-racking because I am the little

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:16.300
sister.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.880
I am so used to [us] always going through
these challenging moments in our career together,

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.380
[but] I am beginning to understand that this
film is so much bigger than me.”

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.820
This is the first time Bailey has publicly
addressed the criticism… but in July, she

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.340
revealed that she's overjoyed about landing
the role.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.800
"It's really, really exciting, you know?

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.610
I just feel really grateful right now.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.200
I'm still really in shock.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.120
I just found out a day or two ago."

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.320
Unfortunately, the backlash hasn't entirely
settled yet.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.160
One fan even started an online petition, writing
that:

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.100
"We feel the casting is a betrayal of Ariel's
original creators, die-hard fans and small

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.350
children the world over who just want to see
the Ariel they already know and love brought

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.600
to life for the big screen."

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.420
Of course, plenty of people are praising the
casting choice — including Jodi Benson,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.760
who voiced Ariel in the animated movie, its
sequels, and a number of television projects.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.340
Benson defended Bailey's casting by saying:

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.320
"I think that the spirit of a character is
what really matters.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.160
What you bring to the table in a character
as far as their heart, and their spirit, is

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.200
what really counts.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.780
[…] No matter what we look like on the outside,
no matter our race, our nation, [or] the color

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:20.300
of our skin."

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.560
On July 6th, the Disney-owned cable network
Freeform published an open letter on Instagram

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.580
firmly standing by the casting choice.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:28.990
The post read in part:

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.760
"The character of Ariel is a work of fiction.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.770
So after all of this is said and done, and
you still cannot get past the idea that choosing

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:40.590
the incredible, sensational, highly-talented,
gorgeous Halle Bailey is anything other than

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:45.240
the INSPIRED casting that it is because she
'doesn't look like the cartoon one,' oh boy,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.520
do I have some news for you… about you."

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:52.640
Director Rob Marshall also voiced his unwavering
faith in Bailey's talent, telling Variety:

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.920
"[She possesses a] rare combination of spirit,
heart, youth, innocence, and substance — plus

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.880
a glorious singing voice — all intrinsic
qualities necessary to play this iconic role."

